 <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How do you compare rewards credit cards?</h3><p>Match each card's bonus categories to where you actually spend, subtract the annual fee from expected rewards, then compare sign-up bonuses and how easily you can redeem.</p><h3>Is cash back or points better?</h3><p>Cash back is simpler and always worth face value. Points can be worth more for travel but require effort — choose based on how much optimizing you'll actually do.</p><h3>Is an annual-fee card worth it?</h3><p>Only if your rewards minus the fee beat a no-fee card. For most beginners a no-fee flat-rate card wins until spending is high enough to justify a fee.</p><h3>Does APR matter if I pay in full?</h3><p>No — if you pay the full statement balance every month you never pay interest, so you can ignore APR and optimize purely for rewards.</p><h3>How do sign-up bonuses work?</h3><p>You earn a lump reward after spending a set amount in the first few months. Only chase one you'd hit through normal spending, never by overspending.</p><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@graph": [{"@type": "BlogPosting", "headline": "How to Compare Rewards Credit Cards (Beginners)", "about": "how to compare rewards credit cards", "url": "https://learners-link.blogspot.com/2026/09/how-to-compare-rewards-credit-cards-for.html", "dateModified": "2026-09-15", "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Learner's Link"}, "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Learner's Link"}}, {"@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "How do you compare rewards credit cards?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Match each card's bonus categories to where you actually spend, subtract the annual fee from expected rewards, then compare sign-up bonuses and how easily you can redeem."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Is cash back or points better?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Cash back is simpler and always worth fac